    The file is named for how it is used -- one test, changed as we go. The
    standing downside is that the changers have been reluctant to clean up as
    they go, so scratch accumulates below the stop.

    ⚠ WHAT THE BASELINE CERTIFIES, AND IT IS THE WHOLE OF IT:
    genLadder/oneTest.base certifies THE FIVE generateAction ROWS ABOVE AND
    NOTHING BELOW THIS LINE. Nothing under this stop has ever executed under it.
    Verified by marker: "hello world", "dumpBC for", "testGXLeaf", "Unit Tests"
    and "printDefinition" all appear ZERO times in a full run.

    ⚠ THE FIVE OTHER stop() CALLS ARE GONE, deleted 2026-08-04 under Tony's
    ruling, and the code they were stopping on is KEPT. The general law:

        A SCRATCH OR TEST FILE HAS AT MOST ONE stop() -- THE LIVE BOUNDARY.
        Content below it is explicitly parked WIP with a decision owed at
        completion. Multiple stops are always a smell: each one below the first
        is dead code wearing a seatbelt.

    Deleting them changes NOTHING about what runs -- this stop is still the
    boundary -- and it removes the trap that made the old shape dangerous:
    moving or deleting the boundary to run more did NOT run more, it handed
    control to the next stop a few lines down, which truncated again, silently,
    at exit 0. That is RULE H2's own worked example (incant/jiquery had three
    stops and answered one question out of three for a month). Now, extending
    the live section runs everything you uncover, which is what a reader
    reasonably expects.

    ⚠ PARKED-BELOW-THE-STOP IS A LEGITIMATE STATE ONLY WHILE THE WORK IS
    GENUINELY IN PROCESS. When it completes, a decision is owed: clean it out,
    or promote it somewhere permanent. It does not get to live down there
    forever. Five sections are parked below; the inventory is in the commit that
    carried this edit and the keep-or-clean call is Tony's.
*****************************************************************************/
